[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Distributed Locking & Consensus Algorithms (Raft, Paxos)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In modern distributed systems, multiple nodes must coordinate access to shared resources or agree on a single state. This tutorial explores the theoretical foundations and practical implementations of distributed locking and consensus algorithms, focusing on Paxos and Raft."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Need for Consensus"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consensus is the fundamental problem of agreeing on a single data value among a group of processes. In distributed architectures, network partitions, node crashes, and message delays make this a complex challenge. Without consensus, distributed databases cannot guarantee linearizability or fault tolerance."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Paxos: The Gold Standard of Consensus"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Paxos, introduced by Leslie Lamport, is the most well-known family of consensus protocols. It operates on the principle of quorums. A basic consensus instance relies on two phases: Prepare/Promise and Accept/Accepted."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phase 1a (Prepare): A proposer selects a unique proposal number (n) and sends a prepare request to a quorum of acceptors."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phase 1b (Promise): If n is higher than any previously seen proposal number, acceptors promise not to accept future proposals numbered less than n and return the highest-numbered proposal they have accepted so far."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phase 2a (Accept): If the proposer receives promises from a quorum, it sends an accept request with value v (where v is the value of the highest-numbered proposal among the responses, or any value if none were returned)."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phase 2b (Accepted): Acceptors accept the proposal unless they have already promised to consider a higher-numbered proposal."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Raft: Understandability and Decomposed Consensus"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Raft was designed as an alternative to Paxos to improve understandability. It decomposes consensus into distinct sub-problems: Leader Election, Log Replication, and Safety."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leader Election: Raft uses randomized timers to elect a single leader. If followers do not hear from the leader within an election timeout, they increment their term and start a new election."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Log Replication: The leader accepts client write requests, appends them to its log, and issues AppendEntries RPCs to replicate the log entries across all followers."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Safety: Raft ensures that a leader is always elected with all committed entries, preventing divergent log histories."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Distributed Locking Mechanisms"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"While consensus algorithms manage cluster state, distributed locks manage exclusive access to resources across distinct services. Implementing a distributed lock naively using a single database row often fails due to network partitions and failovers."},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Redlock algorithm, designed for Redis in distributed environments, attempts to solve this by acquiring locks across a quorum of independent Redis master nodes."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"// Conceptual Python example for acquiring a distributed lock
def acquire_lock(client, lock_key, acquire_timeout=10):
    identifier = str(uuid.uuid4())
    end = time.time() + acquire_timeout
    while time.time() < end:
        if client.set(lock_key, identifier, nx=True, px=30000):
            return identifier
        time.sleep(0.001)
    return False"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing between Paxos, Raft, and distributed locking strategies depends on system requirements. Raft is favored for state machine replication in systems like etcd, while Paxos underpins Google's Spanner. Always evaluate network latency, fault tolerance thresholds, and consistency models before designing your architecture."}]
